---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="The Et Hunter — Daf Yomi Game: Bekhorot 6"
  description="Bekhorot 6: Shimon HaAmasoni expounded every little 'et' in the Torah — until he reached 'Et the Lord your God you shall fear.' Hunt the particles yourself, build a tower of proofs that milk is kosher and watch which ones crack, then climb a ladder of strange-calf dilemmas the Gemara never quite settles."
>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;500;700;900&display=swap"
  />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#12141f] !text-[#ece6d6]">
    <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
    <div class="border-b border-[#262a3d] bg-gradient-to-b from-[#1a1d2c]/80 to-transparent px-4 pt-10 pb-8 text-center">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] !text-[#d9a441]">
        <a href="/daf" class="hover:!text-[#f0c060]">daf yomi arcade</a> · bekhorot 6 · 2026-09-27
      </p>
      <h1 class="frank text-4xl font-black tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">🥛 The Et Hunter</h1>
      <p class="frank mt-1 text-xl !text-[#f0c060]" dir="rtl" lang="he">כְּשֵׁם שֶׁקִּבַּלְתִּי שָׂכָר עַל הַדְּרִישָׁה, כָּךְ אֲנִי מְקַבֵּל שָׂכָר עַל הַפְּרִישָׁה</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#9aa0b4]">
        Shimon HaAmasoni took every little <span class="frank !text-[#ece6d6]" dir="rtl" lang="he">אֵת</span> in the
        Torah — the untranslatable word that marks a direct object — as a hint to add one more law. Then he
        reached a verse where there was nothing left to add.
        <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">Hunt the particles the way he did</strong>, then stack the Gemara's proofs
        that milk is even kosher and see which ones hold, and finally climb a ladder of strange-calf questions
        from <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Bekhorot.6a.10" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="!text-[#f0c060] underline decoration-[#4a4028] hover:!text-[#ffd98a]">6a</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex justify-center gap-3 text-xs">
        <span class="rounded-full border border-[#3a3420] bg-[#1a1d2c] px-3 py-1 !text-[#d9a441]">שָׂכָר עַל הַדְּרִישָׁה <span id="coin-drash" class="num font-bold">0</span></span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-[#2a3a36] bg-[#1a1d2c] px-3 py-1 !text-[#8fcfb8]">שָׂכָר עַל הַפְּרִישָׁה <span id="coin-prisha" class="num font-bold">0</span></span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8">
      <!-- ============ Field notes ============ -->
      <details class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#262a3d] bg-[#1a1d2c] p-5">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d9a441]">
          field notes — how the daf opens (6a:1–9) ▾
        </summary>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#9aa0b4]">
          <p>
            The page opens on why only a <em>donkey's</em> firstborn is holy, not a horse's or camel's. Rabbi
            Yosei HaGelili pointed to "firstborn of a donkey" appearing twice. Rav Aḥai pushes back: the second
            mention might only mean horses and camels aren't redeemed <em>with a lamb</em>, while still needing
            redemption with something else. The answer: then the Torah would have phrased it differently the
            second time. Repeating the exact same words means only donkeys count.
          </p>
          <p>
            The mishna's tanna already uses that repetition for another rule, so Rav Pappa gets there by a
            different route: "all your livestock… ox and lamb… and the firstborn of a donkey" is a
            <em>generalization followed by details</em> (<span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">כְּלָל וּפְרָט</span>),
            and a generalization covers only the details listed. Rabbi Yosei HaGelili answers that the word
            "firstborn" breaks the donkey off from that list. The Rabbis answer that the letter <em>vav</em> ("and")
            joins it back on, and that "firstborn" has to stand between them anyway, because an ox or lamb goes
            on the altar while a donkey is redeemed for its value.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- ============ Part I: the Et hunt ============ -->
      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-[#3a3420] bg-[#1a1d2c] p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d9a441]">part i · 6b:3–5</p>
        <h2 class="frank mt-1 text-2xl font-bold">Every <span dir="rtl" lang="he">אֵת</span> and <span dir="rtl" lang="he">אֵת</span></h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#9aa0b4]">
          <span class="frank !text-[#cfc7ae]" dir="rtl" lang="he">״שִׁמְעוֹן הָעַמְסוֹנִי הָיָה דּוֹרֵשׁ כׇּל ״אֵת״ וָ״אֵת״ שֶׁבַּתּוֹרָה״</span>.
          Tap every <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">אֶת</strong> in each verse to expound it. A wrong tap costs you
          a coin. The last verse is where he stopped.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-xs" id="verse-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Verses"></div>

        <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[#262a3d] bg-[#12141f] p-4">
          <p id="verse-ref" class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#6d7389]"></p>
          <p id="verse-text" class="frank mt-2 text-2xl leading-[2.4] sm:text-3xl" dir="rtl" lang="he"></p>
          <p id="verse-gloss" class="mt-2 text-xs italic !text-[#6d7389]"></p>
          <p id="verse-status" class="mt-3 min-h-[1.25rem] text-sm !text-[#9aa0b4]" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>

        <!-- The moment of withdrawal -->
        <div id="withdraw-box" class="mt-4 hidden rounded-xl border border-[#4a3a20] bg-[#1f1a12] p-4">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#ece6d6]">
            <strong class="!text-[#f0c060]">"You shall fear <em>et</em> the Lord your God."</strong> The particle is
            right there. What does it add?
          </p>
          <div class="mt-3 grid gap-2" id="withdraw-choices"></div>
          <div id="withdraw-reveal" class="mt-3 hidden space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#cfc7ae]"></div>
        </div>

        <!-- Who expounds what -->
        <div id="camel-box" class="mt-6 hidden rounded-xl border border-[#262a3d] bg-[#12141f] p-4">
          <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d9a441]">the camel, twice · 6b:2–3</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#9aa0b4]">
            "Camel" is forbidden in both Leviticus 11:4 and Deuteronomy 14:7. The Rabbis side with Shimon HaAmasoni's
            withdrawal and <em>don't</em> expound <span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">אֵת</span>. Rabbi Shimon still does.
            Who uses which source for what? Tap a sage for each row.
          </p>
          <div id="camel-rows" class="mt-3 space-y-2"></div>
          <p id="camel-status" class="mt-3 min-h-[1.25rem] text-sm !text-[#9aa0b4]" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Part II: the milk tower ============ -->
      <section class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#3a3a44] bg-[#1a1d2c] p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#e8e2d0]">part ii · 6b:6–18</p>
        <h2 class="frank mt-1 text-2xl font-bold">Is milk even kosher?</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#9aa0b4]">
          Rav Ashi explains why a camel's milk needed its own prohibition: milk being allowed at all is a
          surprise. One view is that milk is <em>blood that has turned</em>, since a nursing mother doesn't
          menstruate. The other is that it comes from a living animal, and nothing else from a living animal is
          allowed. So how do we know even a cow's milk is allowed? The Gemara proposes six proofs. For each one, decide:
          <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">does it hold, or does it crack?</strong> If it cracks, pick the Gemara's objection.
          Proofs that hold go on the tower.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-5 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-[1fr_9rem]">
          <div>
            <div id="proof-card" class="rounded-xl border border-[#3a3a44] bg-[#12141f] p-4"></div>
            <p id="proof-progress" class="mt-2 text-xs !text-[#6d7389]"></p>
          </div>
          <div class="flex flex-col items-center">
            <p class="mb-2 text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#6d7389]">the tower</p>
            <div id="tower" class="flex h-72 w-32 flex-col-reverse items-stretch gap-1 rounded-b-lg border-b-4 border-[#d9a441]/60 p-1" aria-label="Proofs that held"></div>
            <div id="rubble" class="mt-3 flex w-32 flex-wrap justify-center gap-1" aria-label="Proofs that cracked"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Part III: the dilemma ladder ============ -->
      <section class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#2a3a36] bg-[#1a1d2c] p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#8fcfb8]">part iii · 6a:10–19</p>
        <h2 class="frank mt-1 text-2xl font-bold">The ladder of strange calves</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#9aa0b4]">
          A ewe that bears a goat-like kid is a firstborn if the kid shares <em>some</em> features with its mother.
          But ewe and goat are both kosher and both go on the altar. The Gemara asks whether that rule reaches further,
          one case at a time, each question starting "and if you say yes…" (<span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">אִם תִּמְצֵי לוֹמַר</span>).
          Two baraitot are brought as proof. The Gemara reads each one as <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">narrowly as
          it can</strong>. Tap the rungs a baraita <em>must</em> cover, and nothing more.
        </p>

        <div id="ladder" class="mt-5 space-y-2"></div>

        <div id="baraita-box" class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-[#262a3d] bg-[#12141f] p-4"></div>

        <div id="red-donkey" class="mt-5 hidden rounded-xl border border-[#4a2a26] bg-[#1f1614] p-4">
          <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#e39a80]">the red donkey · 6a:17–19</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#cfc7ae]">
            If the second baraita's "some features" clause is only about the cow, why does it mention a donkey
            bearing a horse-like foal at all? That the foal is exempt when it <em>lacks</em> the mother's features
            seems obvious, since it's even more obvious than the cow case. First compare the animals: tap the two
            that look alike.
          </p>
          <div id="beasts" class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-3 gap-2"></div>
          <div id="red-choices" class="mt-4 hidden grid gap-2"></div>
          <div id="red-reveal" class="mt-3 hidden text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#cfc7ae]"></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="finale" class="mt-10 hidden rounded-2xl border border-[#3a3420] bg-gradient-to-b from-[#1f1a12] to-[#1a1d2c] p-6 text-center">
        <p class="frank text-2xl font-bold !text-[#f0c060]">הֲדַרַן עֲלָךְ</p>
        <p id="finale-text" class="mt-2 text-sm !text-[#cfc7ae]"></p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Learn the real daf ============ -->
      <div class="mt-14 border-t border-[#262a3d] pt-8">
        <h3 class="frank mb-3 text-xl font-bold !text-[#d9a441]">Learn the real daf</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#9aa0b4]">
          Bekhorot 6 moves from which species have firstborn sanctity at all (Rav Pappa's generalization-and-detail
          reading) to the offspring whose species doesn't match its mother's. For eating, the rule is simple: go by
          the mother. "What comes from the impure is impure." The one exception is Rabbi Shimon, who reads "camel,
          camel" to forbid even a camel born to a cow. That leads into Shimon HaAmasoni's <em>et</em>, the proofs
          that milk is kosher, and the Torah's second list of forbidden animals, repeated only for the
          <em>shesu'a</em> (an animal with two backs and two spines) and the bird called <em>ra'a</em>. The daf ends
          with Rabbi Shimon's stricter test for firstborn status: the head and most of the body must look like the mother.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-xs">
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Bekhorot.6a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-full border border-[#3a3420] bg-[#1a1d2c]/60 px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#d9a441] hover:border-[#d9a441]">Bekhorot 6 on Sefaria ↗</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rashi_on_Bekhorot.6a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-full border border-[#262a3d] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#9aa0b4] hover:border-[#d9a441]">Rashi ×40 ↗</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Steinsaltz_on_Bekhorot.6a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-full border border-[#262a3d] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#9aa0b4] hover:border-[#d9a441]">Steinsaltz ×46 ↗</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rabbeinu_Gershom_on_Bekhorot.6a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-full border border-[#262a3d] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#9aa0b4] hover:border-[#d9a441]">Rabbeinu Gershom ×40 ↗</a>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-[#262a3d] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#9aa0b4]">Responsa Chatam Sofer ×11</span>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-[#262a3d] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#9aa0b4]">Tosafot ×8</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 text-xs !text-[#5c6278]">
          Part of the <a href="/daf" class="underline decoration-[#3a3f4d] hover:!text-[#9aa0b4]">Daf Yomi Arcade</a> —
          a new game from every daf. Texts and links via the Sefaria API.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style is:global>
    .frank { font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif; }
    .num { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

    .word-btn {
      border-radius: 0.4rem;
      padding: 0 0.2rem;
      color: #ece6d6;
      transition: background-color 0.15s, color 0.15s;
    }
    .word-btn:hover:not(:disabled) { background: #262a3d; }
    .word-btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #d9a441; outline-offset: 2px; }
    .word-btn.hit { background: #d9a441; color: #12141f !important; }
    .word-btn.miss { background: #4a2420; color: #f0b8a8 !important; }
    .word-btn:disabled { cursor: default; }

    .choice-btn {
      text-align: left;
      border-radius: 0.7rem;
      border: 1px solid #3a3f52;
      background: #12141f;
      color: #cfc7ae;
      padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      transition: background-color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s, color 0.15s;
    }
    .choice-btn:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: #d9a441; color: #ece6d6; }
    .choice-btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #d9a441; outline-offset: 2px; }
    .choice-btn:disabled { cursor: default; }
    .choice-btn.ok { border-color: #5f9c88; background: #15241f; color: #bfe8d8 !important; }
    .choice-btn.bad { border-color: #b25b3c; background: #241713; color: #f0c3b0 !important; }

    .pill {
      border-radius: 999px;
      border: 1px solid #3a3f52;
      background: #12141f;
      color: #9aa0b4;
      padding: 0.35rem 0.85rem;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      transition: background-color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s, color 0.15s;
    }
    .pill:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: #d9a441; color: #ece6d6; }
    .pill:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #d9a441; outline-offset: 2px; }
    .pill[aria-pressed='true'], .pill[aria-selected='true'] { background: #d9a441; border-color: #d9a441; color: #12141f !important; }
    .pill.ok { background: #5f9c88; border-color: #5f9c88; color: #0e1613 !important; }
    .pill.bad { background: #b25b3c; border-color: #b25b3c; color: #1c0f0b !important; }
    .pill.done::after { content: ' ✓'; }

    .rung {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr auto;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.75rem;
      width: 100%;
      text-align: left;
      border-radius: 0.8rem;
      border: 1px solid #2f3446;
      background: #12141f;
      padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
      transition: border-color 0.15s, background-color 0.15s;
    }
    .rung:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: #8fcfb8; }
    .rung:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #8fcfb8; outline-offset: 2px; }
    .rung[aria-pressed='true'] { border-color: #8fcfb8; background: #15241f; }
    .rung:disabled { cursor: default; }

    .brick {
      border-radius: 0.3rem;
      background: linear-gradient(#f3efe3, #d8d0b8);
      color: #1a1d2c !important;
      font-size: 0.65rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      padding: 0.35rem 0.3rem;
      text-align: center;
      animation: drop 0.45s cubic-bezier(0.3, 1.4, 0.6, 1);
    }
    .shard {
      border-radius: 0.2rem;
      background: #3a2a24;
      color: #e39a80 !important;
      font-size: 0.6rem;
      padding: 0.15rem 0.35rem;
      animation: crumble 0.5s ease-out;
    }
    .coin-pop { animation: pop 0.35s ease-out; }
    @keyframes drop { from { transform: translateY(-120px); opacity: 0; } to { transform: none; opacity: 1; } }
    @keyframes crumble { from { transform: rotate(-12deg) translateY(-20px); opacity: 0; } to { transform: none; opacity: 1; } }
    @keyframes pop { 50% { transform: scale(1.5); } }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .brick, .shard, .coin-pop { animation: none !important; }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id)!;
    const sef = (ref: string) => `https://www.sefaria.org/${ref.replace(/ (\d+[ab]?):/, '.$1.').replace(/ /g, '_')}`;
    const link = (ref: string) =>
      `<a href="${sef(ref)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="!text-[#d9a441] hover:!text-[#f0c060] hover:underline">${ref} ↗</a>`;

    // ---------- coins ----------
    let drash = 0;
    let prisha = 0;
    const bump = (el: HTMLElement) => {
      el.classList.remove('coin-pop');
      void el.offsetWidth;
      el.classList.add('coin-pop');
    };
    const setCoins = () => {
      $('coin-drash').textContent = String(drash);
      $('coin-prisha').textContent = String(prisha);
    };

    // =============== PART I: the et hunt ===============
    type Word = { t: string; et?: boolean; gloss?: string };
    type Verse = { ref: string; label: string; words: Word[]; gloss: string; final?: boolean };

    const VERSES: Verse[] = [
      {
        ref: 'Exodus 20:12',
        label: 'Honor your parents',
        gloss: 'Honor your father and your mother, so that your days are lengthened on the land…',
        words: [
          { t: 'כַּבֵּד' },
          { t: 'אֶת', et: true, gloss: 'Ketubot 103a: "et your father" adds your father\'s wife (your stepmother).' },
          { t: 'אָבִיךָ' },
          { t: 'וְאֶת', et: true, gloss: 'Ketubot 103a: "ve-et your mother" adds your mother\'s husband, and the extra vav adds your older brother.' },
          { t: 'אִמֶּךָ' },
          { t: 'לְמַעַן' },
          { t: 'יַאֲרִכוּן' },
          { t: 'יָמֶיךָ' },
          { t: 'עַל' },
          { t: 'הָאֲדָמָה' },
        ],
      },
      {
        ref: 'Leviticus 11:4',
        label: 'The camel',
        gloss: 'But these you shall not eat, of those that chew the cud or split the hoof: the camel, for it chews the cud but does not split the hoof — it is impure for you.',
        words: [
          { t: 'אַךְ' },
          { t: 'אֶת', et: true, gloss: 'Every et counts for Shimon HaAmasoni, even the one in "et zeh". This is the same verse the daf uses (6a:22) to show that a kosher-looking animal born to a non-kosher mother is still forbidden.' },
          { t: 'זֶה' },
          { t: 'לֹא' },
          { t: 'תֹאכְלוּ' },
          { t: 'מִמַּעֲלֵי' },
          { t: 'הַגֵּרָה' },
          { t: 'וּמִמַּפְרִסֵי' },
          { t: 'הַפַּרְסָה' },
          { t: 'אֶת', et: true, gloss: 'Bekhorot 6b:3: Rabbi Shimon reads "et ha-gamal" as adding the camel\'s milk.' },
          { t: 'הַגָּמָל' },
          { t: 'כִּי' },
          { t: 'מַעֲלֵה' },
          { t: 'גֵרָה' },
          { t: 'הוּא' },
        ],
      },
      {
        ref: 'Leviticus 11:31',
        label: 'Bonus: the extra heh',
        gloss: 'These are the impure [ha-teme\'im] for you among all the creeping things… (Here the hint isn\'t an et. Find the one extra letter.)',
        words: [
          { t: 'אֵלֶּה' },
          { t: 'הַ', et: true, gloss: 'Bekhorot 6b:7: the heh of "ha-teme\'im" forbids their juice [tzir], their gravy [rotev], and the congealed sediment [kifa]. So a creeping thing\'s fluids are forbidden too, which is exactly why Rav Aḥa asks why camel\'s milk needed a verse at all.' },
          { t: 'טְּמֵאִים' },
          { t: 'לָכֶם' },
          { t: 'בְּכׇל' },
          { t: 'הַשָּׁרֶץ' },
        ],
      },
      {
        ref: 'Deuteronomy 6:13',
        label: 'The fear of God',
        gloss: 'You shall fear the Lord your God…',
        final: true,
        words: [
          { t: 'אֶת', et: true },
          { t: 'ה׳' },
          { t: 'אֱלֹהֶיךָ' },
          { t: 'תִּירָא' },
        ],
      },
    ];

    let verseIdx = 0;
    const found: boolean[][] = VERSES.map((v) => v.words.map(() => false));
    const missed: boolean[][] = VERSES.map((v) => v.words.map(() => false));
    const verseDone = (i: number) => VERSES[i].words.every((w, j) => !w.et || found[i][j]);

    function renderTabs() {
      const tabs = $('verse-tabs');
      tabs.innerHTML = '';
      VERSES.forEach((v, i) => {
        const b = document.createElement('button');
        b.className = 'pill' + (verseDone(i) ? ' done' : '');
        b.setAttribute('role', 'tab');
        b.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(i === verseIdx));
        const locked = v.final && !VERSES.slice(0, -1).every((_, k) => verseDone(k));
        b.disabled = !!locked;
        b.textContent = locked ? `🔒 ${v.label}` : v.label;
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          verseIdx = i;
          renderVerse();
        });
        tabs.appendChild(b);
      });
    }

    function renderVerse() {
      renderTabs();
      const v = VERSES[verseIdx];
      $('verse-ref').textContent = v.ref;
      $('verse-gloss').textContent = v.gloss;
      const box = $('verse-text');
      box.innerHTML = '';
      v.words.forEach((w, j) => {
        const b = document.createElement('button');
        b.className = 'word-btn' + (found[verseIdx][j] ? ' hit' : missed[verseIdx][j] ? ' miss' : '');
        b.textContent = w.t;
        b.disabled = found[verseIdx][j] || missed[verseIdx][j] || verseDone(verseIdx);
        b.addEventListener('click', () => tapWord(j));
        box.appendChild(b);
        // the heh is attached to its word
        if (!(w.t === 'הַ' && verseIdx === 2)) box.appendChild(document.createTextNode(' '));
      });
      $('verse-status').innerHTML = verseDone(verseIdx)
        ? `All expounded. ${link(v.ref)}`
        : `${v.words.filter((w, j) => w.et && !found[verseIdx][j]).length} left to find.`;
    }

    function tapWord(j: number) {
      const v = VERSES[verseIdx];
      const w = v.words[j];
      if (w.et) {
        found[verseIdx][j] = true;
        if (v.final) {
          renderVerse();
          openWithdraw();
          return;
        }
        drash++;
        bump($('coin-drash'));
        setCoins();
        renderVerse();
        $('verse-status').innerHTML = `<span class="!text-[#f0c060]">+1 שָׂכָר עַל הַדְּרִישָׁה.</span> ${w.gloss ?? ''}`;
        if (verseDone(verseIdx)) {
          renderTabs();
          const next = VERSES.findIndex((_, k) => !verseDone(k));
          if (next === VERSES.length - 1) {
            $('verse-status').innerHTML += ` <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">One verse left, and it's unlocked.</strong>`;
          }
        }
      } else {
        missed[verseIdx][j] = true;
        drash = Math.max(0, drash - 1);
        setCoins();
        renderVerse();
        $('verse-status').innerHTML = `<span class="!text-[#e39a80]">"${w.t}" isn't the particle.</span> −1 coin.`;
      }
    }

    const WITHDRAW = [
      { t: 'Include parents: fear them as you fear God', ok: false, why: 'Tempting, but parents are already covered: "each person shall fear his mother and father" (Leviticus 19:3). Shimon HaAmasoni saw nothing that belongs next to the fear of God.' },
      { t: 'Include angels in the fear', ok: false, why: 'The daf gives no such reading. The whole point is that Shimon HaAmasoni saw nothing that could stand next to God in fear.' },
      { t: 'Nothing can stand beside God. Withdraw.', ok: true, why: '' },
    ];

    function openWithdraw() {
      $('withdraw-box').classList.remove('hidden');
      const wrap = $('withdraw-choices');
      wrap.innerHTML = '';
      WITHDRAW.forEach((c) => {
        const b = document.createElement('button');
        b.className = 'choice-btn';
        b.textContent = c.t;
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (!c.ok) {
            b.classList.add('bad');
            b.disabled = true;
            b.textContent = `${c.t} — ${c.why}`;
            return;
          }
          wrap.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((x) => ((x as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true));
          b.classList.add('ok');
          prisha = drash; // "just as I received reward for the exposition…"
          bump($('coin-prisha'));
          setCoins();
          const r = $('withdraw-reveal');
          r.classList.remove('hidden');
          r.innerHTML = `
            <p><span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">״כֵּיוָן שֶׁהִגִּיעַ לְ׳אֶת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ תִּירָא׳ פֵּירַשׁ״</span>. He withdrew. His students asked what would become of all the <em>et</em>s he had already expounded.</p>
            <p class="frank text-lg !text-[#8fcfb8]" dir="rtl" lang="he">״כְּשֵׁם שֶׁקִּבַּלְתִּי שָׂכָר עַל הַדְּרִישָׁה, כָּךְ אֲנִי מְקַבֵּל שָׂכָר עַל הַפְּרִישָׁה״</p>
            <p><em>"Just as I received reward for expounding, so I receive reward for withdrawing."</em> Your withdrawal counter now matches your expounding counter: <strong class="num !text-[#8fcfb8]">${prisha}</strong>.</p>
            <p class="rounded-lg border border-[#3a3420] bg-[#12141f] p-3">…<span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">״עַד שֶׁבָּא רַבִּי עֲקִיבָא וְלִימֵּד: ׳אֶת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ תִּירָא׳ — לְרַבּוֹת תַּלְמִידֵי חֲכָמִים״</span>.
            <br />Until Rabbi Akiva came and taught that this <em>et</em> includes <strong class="!text-[#f0c060]">Torah scholars</strong>: you fear them alongside God.</p>
            <p>${link('Bekhorot 6b:4')} · ${link('Bekhorot 6b:5')}</p>`;
          $('verse-status').textContent = 'He stopped here.';
          openCamel();
        });
        wrap.appendChild(b);
      });
    }

    // ----- who expounds what -----
    const CAMEL = [
      { row: '"Camel… camel" → a camel born to a cow is forbidden to eat', who: 'shimon' },
      { row: '"Camel… camel" → one for the camel, one for camel\'s milk', who: 'rabbanan' },
      { row: '"Et ha-gamal" → camel\'s milk is forbidden', who: 'shimon' },
      { row: 'An animal that looks non-kosher but was born to a cow may be eaten', who: 'rabbanan' },
    ];
    let camelRight = 0;
    function openCamel() {
      $('camel-box').classList.remove('hidden');
      const rows = $('camel-rows');
      rows.innerHTML = '';
      CAMEL.forEach((c) => {
        const d = document.createElement('div');
        d.className = 'flex flex-col gap-2 rounded-lg border border-[#262a3d] p-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between';
        d.innerHTML = `<p class="text-sm !text-[#cfc7ae]">${c.row}</p>`;
        const btns = document.createElement('div');
        btns.className = 'flex shrink-0 gap-2';
        [['shimon', 'Rabbi Shimon'], ['rabbanan', 'The Rabbis']].forEach(([k, label]) => {
          const b = document.createElement('button');
          b.className = 'pill';
          b.textContent = label;
          b.addEventListener('click', () => {
            btns.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((x) => ((x as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true));
            if (k === c.who) {
              b.classList.add('ok');
              camelRight++;
            } else b.classList.add('bad');
            const done = rows.querySelectorAll('button:disabled').length === CAMEL.length * 2;
            if (done) {
              $('camel-status').innerHTML = `${camelRight}/4. Everyone agrees camel's milk is forbidden; they only disagree about <em>which</em> word teaches it. And the Rabbis' refusal to expound <em>et</em> comes straight from Shimon HaAmasoni's withdrawal. ${link('Bekhorot 6b:2')} · ${link('Bekhorot 6b:3')}`;
              score.camel = camelRight;
              checkFinale();
            }
          });
          btns.appendChild(b);
        });
        d.appendChild(btns);
        rows.appendChild(d);
      });
    }

    // =============== PART II: the milk tower ===============
    type Proof = {
      label: string;
      brick: string;
      source: string;
      he?: string;
      claim: string;
      holds: boolean;
      objections?: { t: string; ok: boolean }[];
      reveal: string;
      ref: string;
    };
    const PROOFS: Proof[] = [
      {
        label: 'Meat cooked in milk',
        brick: 'meat & milk',
        source: '"Do not cook a kid in its mother\'s milk"',
        claim: 'The Torah forbids meat <em>cooked in</em> milk, so plain milk must be fine.',
        holds: false,
        objections: [
          { t: 'Maybe plain milk is forbidden to eat but fine for benefit; the meat-and-milk mix is also forbidden for benefit.', ok: true },
          { t: 'The verse is about a kid, not a cow.', ok: false },
          { t: 'Meat and milk is only rabbinic.', ok: false },
        ],
        reveal: 'Cracks. Mentioning the mixture could just mean the mixture is forbidden even for <em>benefit</em>. Even Rabbi Shimon, who allows benefit from meat and milk, has a reason for the verse: <em>lashes for cooking</em> them together, which wouldn\'t apply to milk alone.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6b:11',
      },
      {
        label: 'The redeemed offering',
        brick: 'redeemed offering',
        source: '"You may slaughter and eat meat" (Deuteronomy 12:15)',
        claim: 'A disqualified, redeemed offering: "slaughter" but don\'t shear, "meat" but not its milk. So ordinary milk must be allowed.',
        holds: false,
        objections: [
          { t: 'The verse is about hunting animals.', ok: false },
          { t: 'Maybe the difference is only benefit: ordinary milk is fine for benefit, while an offering\'s milk is forbidden even for that.', ok: true },
          { t: 'Redeemed offerings are just like ordinary animals.', ok: false },
        ],
        reveal: 'Cracks. Same objection again: the verse could be separating <em>benefit</em>, not eating.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6b:13',
      },
      {
        label: "Goats' milk for food",
        brick: "Proverbs' goats",
        source: 'Proverbs 27:27',
        he: 'וְדֵי חֲלֵב עִזִּים לְלַחְמְךָ',
        claim: '"Enough goats\' milk for your food, the food of your household…"',
        holds: false,
        objections: [
          { t: 'Proverbs is a later book and can\'t teach halakha.', ok: false },
          { t: 'Goats aren\'t cows.', ok: false },
          { t: 'Maybe you sell the milk and buy food with the money.', ok: true },
        ],
        reveal: 'Cracks. "For your food" could mean food you <em>buy</em> with the milk money.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6b:15',
      },
      {
        label: "David's ten cheeses",
        brick: 'ten cheeses',
        source: 'I Samuel 17:18',
        he: 'וְאֵת עֲשֶׂרֶת חֲרִצֵי הֶחָלָב הָאֵלֶּה תָּבִיא לְשַׂר הָאָלֶף',
        claim: 'Jesse sends David to the front with ten cheeses for the captain of the thousand.',
        holds: true,
        reveal: 'Holds. The Gemara tries the same objection, that the captain would sell them, and then laughs it off: <em>"Is it the norm to do business in wartime?"</em> The cheese was for eating.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6b:17',
      },
      {
        label: 'Milk and honey',
        brick: 'milk & honey',
        source: 'Exodus 3:17',
        he: 'אֶרֶץ זָבַת חָלָב וּדְבָשׁ',
        claim: 'The Land is praised as "flowing with milk and honey."',
        holds: true,
        reveal: 'Holds. Would the Torah praise the Land with something you aren\'t allowed to eat?',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6b:18',
      },
      {
        label: 'Buy wine and milk',
        brick: 'wine & milk',
        source: 'Isaiah 55:1',
        he: 'לְכוּ שִׁבְרוּ וֶאֱכֹלוּ… יַיִן וְחָלָב',
        claim: '"Come, buy and eat… wine and milk, without money and without price."',
        holds: true,
        reveal: 'Holds. The prophet invites you to <em>eat</em>, and milk is on the menu.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6b:18',
      },
    ];
    let proofIdx = 0;
    let proofScore = 0;

    function renderProof() {
      const card = $('proof-card');
      $('proof-progress').textContent = `Proof ${Math.min(proofIdx + 1, PROOFS.length)} of ${PROOFS.length} · ${proofScore} judged right`;
      if (proofIdx >= PROOFS.length) {
        card.innerHTML = `<p class="frank text-lg font-bold !text-[#f3efe3]">Three bricks standing.</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#9aa0b4]">Both legal proofs (the meat-and-milk prohibition and the redeemed-offering rule) crack, and so does Proverbs.
          What holds up is <em>narrative</em>: soldiers eating cheese, a Land praised for its milk, a prophet inviting you to drink. You judged <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">${proofScore}/6</strong> right.</p>`;
        score.milk = proofScore;
        checkFinale();
        return;
      }
      const p = PROOFS[proofIdx];
      card.innerHTML = `
        <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#6d7389]">${p.source}</p>
        <p class="frank mt-1 text-xl font-bold !text-[#f3efe3]">${p.label}</p>
        ${p.he ? `<p class="frank mt-2 text-lg !text-[#cfc7ae]" dir="rtl" lang="he">${p.he}</p>` : ''}
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm !text-[#9aa0b4]">${p.claim}</p>
        <div class="mt-3 flex gap-2" id="hc-btns">
          <button class="pill" data-v="hold">🧱 It holds</button>
          <button class="pill" data-v="crack">💥 It cracks</button>
        </div>
        <div id="obj" class="mt-3 hidden grid gap-2"></div>
        <div id="proof-reveal" class="mt-3 hidden text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#cfc7ae]"></div>`;
      card.querySelectorAll('#hc-btns button').forEach((b) =>
        b.addEventListener('click', () => judge((b as HTMLElement).dataset.v === 'hold', b as HTMLButtonElement)),
      );
    }

    function judge(saidHold: boolean, btn: HTMLButtonElement) {
      const p = PROOFS[proofIdx];
      $('hc-btns').querySelectorAll('button').forEach((x) => ((x as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true));
      const right = saidHold === p.holds;
      btn.classList.add(right ? 'ok' : 'bad');
      if (!right) return finishProof(false);
      if (p.holds) return finishProof(true);
      const obj = $('obj');
      obj.classList.remove('hidden');
      obj.innerHTML = `<p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.15em] !text-[#e39a80]">What's the Gemara's objection?</p>`;
      p.objections!.forEach((o) => {
        const b = document.createElement('button');
        b.className = 'choice-btn';
        b.textContent = o.t;
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          obj.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((x) => ((x as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true));
          b.classList.add(o.ok ? 'ok' : 'bad');
          if (!o.ok) obj.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((x, i) => p.objections![i].ok && x.classList.add('ok'));
          finishProof(o.ok);
        });
        obj.appendChild(b);
      });
    }

    function finishProof(right: boolean) {
      const p = PROOFS[proofIdx];
      if (right) proofScore++;
      const r = $('proof-reveal');
      r.classList.remove('hidden');
      r.innerHTML = `${right ? '' : '<strong class="!text-[#e39a80]">Not quite.</strong> '}${p.reveal} ${link(p.ref)}
        <div class="mt-3"><button id="next-proof" class="pill">${proofIdx + 1 < PROOFS.length ? 'Next proof →' : 'See the tower'}</button></div>`;
      const el = document.createElement('div');
      if (p.holds) {
        el.className = 'brick';
        el.textContent = p.brick;
        $('tower').appendChild(el);
      } else {
        el.className = 'shard';
        el.textContent = p.brick;
        $('rubble').appendChild(el);
      }
      $('next-proof').addEventListener('click', () => {
        proofIdx++;
        renderProof();
      });
      ($('next-proof') as HTMLButtonElement).focus();
    }

    // =============== PART III: the ladder ===============
    const RUNGS = [
      {
        id: 'cd',
        pair: '🐄 → 🫏',
        name: 'A cow bears a donkey-like calf',
        why: 'Kosher mother, non-kosher-looking offspring. But both cows and donkeys have firstborn sanctity (one inherent, one monetary).',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6a:12',
      },
      {
        id: 'dh',
        pair: '🫏 → 🐎',
        name: 'A donkey bears a horse-like foal',
        why: 'If you say yes to the cow… both are non-kosher. But a horse has no firstborn sanctity at all.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6a:13',
      },
      {
        id: 'ch',
        pair: '🐄 → 🐎',
        name: 'A cow bears a horse-like foal',
        why: 'If you say yes to the donkey too… this one fails both tests: kosher vs. non-kosher, and sanctified vs. not.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6a:14',
      },
    ];
    const BARAITOT = [
      {
        he: 'בְּהֵמָה טְהוֹרָה שֶׁיָּלְדָה מִין בְּהֵמָה טְמֵאָה — פְּטוּרָה מִן הַבְּכוֹרָה, אִם יֵשׁ בּוֹ מִקְצָת סִימָנִין — חַיֶּיבֶת בִּבְכוֹרָה',
        en: '"A kosher animal that bore a non-kosher-like offspring is exempt. If it has some of the features, it\'s obligated."',
        could: ['cd', 'ch'],
        must: ['cd'],
        reveal: 'The wording could cover a cow bearing a horse-like foal too. But the Gemara answers: <em>"No, it means something like a cow bearing a donkey-like calf,"</em> where both species have firstborn sanctity.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6a:15',
      },
      {
        he: 'פָּרָה שֶׁיָּלְדָה מִין חֲמוֹר, וַחֲמוֹר שֶׁיָּלְדָה מִין סוּס — פְּטוּרָה מִן הַבְּכוֹרָה, אִם יֵשׁ בּוֹ מִקְצָת סִימָנִין — חַיֶּיבֶת בִּבְכוֹרָה',
        en: '"A cow that bore a donkey-like calf, or a donkey that bore a horse-like foal, is exempt. If it has some of the features, it\'s obligated."',
        could: ['cd', 'dh'],
        must: ['cd'],
        reveal: 'Doesn\'t the last clause go back on <em>both</em> cases? The Gemara answers: <em>"No, only on the cow bearing a donkey-like calf."</em> The donkey-and-horse case stays open.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 6a:16',
      },
    ];
    let barIdx = 0;
    let picked = new Set<string>();
    const resolved = new Set<string>();
    let ladderScore = 0;

    function renderLadder(interactive: boolean) {
      const l = $('ladder');
      l.innerHTML = '';
      RUNGS.forEach((r, i) => {
        const b = document.createElement('button');
        b.className = 'rung';
        b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(picked.has(r.id)));
        b.disabled = !interactive;
        const status = resolved.has(r.id)
          ? '<span class="text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] !text-[#8fcfb8]">resolved: firstborn</span>'
          : '<span class="text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] !text-[#6d7389]">open</span>';
        b.innerHTML = `<span class="text-2xl" aria-hidden="true">${r.pair}</span>
          <span><span class="block text-sm font-bold !text-[#ece6d6]">Rung ${i + 1}: ${r.name}, with some of its mother's features</span>
          <span class="block text-xs !text-[#9aa0b4]">${r.why}</span></span>${status}`;
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          picked.has(r.id) ? picked.delete(r.id) : picked.add(r.id);
          renderLadder(true);
        });
        l.appendChild(b);
      });
    }

    function renderBaraita() {
      const box = $('baraita-box');
      if (barIdx >= BARAITOT.length) {
        renderLadder(false);
        box.innerHTML = `<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#cfc7ae]">Both baraitot get read down to the first rung. A cow's donkey-like calf with some of its mother's features <strong class="!text-[#8fcfb8]">is a firstborn</strong>. The donkey's horse-like foal and the cow's horse-like foal <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">are left unresolved</strong> on this daf. Ladder reading: <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">${ladderScore}/2</strong>.</p>`;
        score.ladder = ladderScore;
        $('red-donkey').classList.remove('hidden');
        renderBeasts();
        return;
      }
      const br = BARAITOT[barIdx];
      picked = new Set();
      renderLadder(true);
      box.innerHTML = `
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#8fcfb8]">"come and hear" (תָּא שְׁמַע) · baraita ${barIdx + 1} of 2</p>
        <p class="frank mt-2 text-lg !text-[#cfc7ae]" dir="rtl" lang="he">${br.he}</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm !text-[#9aa0b4]">${br.en}</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs !text-[#6d7389]">Select the rungs this baraita <em>must</em> cover on the Gemara's narrowest reading, then submit.</p>
        <button id="bar-submit" class="pill mt-3">Submit reading</button>
        <div id="bar-reveal" class="mt-3 hidden text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#cfc7ae]"></div>`;
      $('bar-submit').addEventListener('click', () => {
        const ok = picked.size === br.must.length && br.must.every((m) => picked.has(m));
        const extra = [...picked].filter((p) => !br.must.includes(p));
        if (ok) ladderScore++;
        ($('bar-submit') as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
        br.must.forEach((m) => resolved.add(m));
        picked = new Set(br.must);
        renderLadder(false);
        const rev = $('bar-reveal');
        rev.classList.remove('hidden');
        rev.innerHTML = `${ok ? '<strong class="!text-[#8fcfb8]">Exactly the Gemara\'s reading.</strong>' : extra.length ? '<strong class="!text-[#e39a80]">Too broad.</strong>' : '<strong class="!text-[#e39a80]">Too narrow.</strong>'} ${br.reveal} ${link(br.ref)}
          <div class="mt-3"><button id="bar-next" class="pill">${barIdx + 1 < BARAITOT.length ? 'Next baraita →' : 'Continue'}</button></div>`;
        $('bar-next').addEventListener('click', () => {
          barIdx++;
          picked = new Set();
          renderBaraita();
        });
      });
    }

    // ----- red donkey -----
    const BEASTS = [
      { id: 'cow', e: '🐄', n: 'Cow', traits: 'horns · split hooves' },
      { id: 'donkey', e: '🫏', n: 'Donkey', traits: 'no horns · closed hooves' },
      { id: 'horse', e: '🐎', n: 'Horse', traits: 'no horns · closed hooves' },
    ];
    const beastPick = new Set<string>();
    let beastsDone = false;
    function renderBeasts() {
      const g = $('beasts');
      g.innerHTML = '';
      BEASTS.forEach((b) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.className = 'choice-btn flex flex-col items-center text-center';
        btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(beastPick.has(b.id)));
        if (beastPick.has(b.id)) btn.style.borderColor = '#e39a80';
        btn.disabled = beastsDone;
        btn.innerHTML = `<span class="text-3xl" aria-hidden="true">${b.e}</span><span class="font-bold">${b.n}</span><span class="text-[11px] !text-[#9aa0b4]">${beastsDone ? b.traits : '?'}</span>`;
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          beastPick.has(b.id) ? beastPick.delete(b.id) : beastPick.add(b.id);
          if (beastPick.size === 2) {
            beastsDone = true;
            const ok = beastPick.has('donkey') && beastPick.has('horse');
            renderBeasts();
            const rc = $('red-choices');
            rc.classList.remove('hidden');
            rc.innerHTML = `<p class="text-sm !text-[#cfc7ae]">${ok ? '<strong class="!text-[#8fcfb8]">Right.</strong>' : '<strong class="!text-[#e39a80]">Look again.</strong>'} Cow and donkey have nothing in common (<em>kelutot</em> means closed hooves). Donkey and horse: no horns, closed hooves. So why does the baraita need the donkey case?</p>`;
            renderRedChoices(ok);
          } else renderBeasts();
        });
        g.appendChild(btn);
      });
    }

    const RED = [
      { t: 'To teach that a horse-like foal with some of its mother\'s features is a firstborn', ok: false },
      { t: 'You might think the foal is just a reddish donkey that looks like a horse, and so a firstborn. The baraita says it\'s a different species.', ok: true },
      { t: 'Because horses have firstborn sanctity too', ok: false },
    ];
    function renderRedChoices(beastOk: boolean) {
      const rc = $('red-choices');
      const wrap = document.createElement('div');
      wrap.className = 'mt-2 grid gap-2';
      RED.forEach((c) => {
        const b = document.createElement('button');
        b.className = 'choice-btn';
        b.textContent = c.t;
        b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          wrap.querySelectorAll('button').forEach((x, i) => {
            (x as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
            if (RED[i].ok) x.classList.add('ok');
          });
          if (!c.ok) b.classList.add('bad');
          score.red = (beastOk ? 1 : 0) + (c.ok ? 1 : 0);
          const r = $('red-reveal');
          r.classList.remove('hidden');
          r.innerHTML = `With a cow, you'd never mistake the calf for a cow, since the two animals are nothing alike. But a donkey and a horse look so similar that <span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">״אֵימָא חֲמוֹר אָדוֹם בְּעָלְמָא הוּא״</span>: you might say "it's just a red donkey" and count it as a firstborn.
            The baraita tells you: <strong class="!text-[#ece6d6]">it's a different species</strong>, not a firstborn. ${link('Bekhorot 6a:19')}
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs !text-[#9aa0b4]">Epilogue (6b:22): Rabbi Shimon won't settle for "some features" at all. For firstborn status he requires the <em>head and most of the body</em> to look like the mother, since the verse says "the firstborn of an <em>ox</em>": it must be an ox. ${link('Bekhorot 6b:24')}</p>`;
          checkFinale();
        });
        wrap.appendChild(b);
      });
      rc.appendChild(wrap);
    }

    // =============== finale ===============
    const score: Record<string, number | undefined> = { camel: undefined, milk: undefined, ladder: undefined, red: undefined };
    function checkFinale() {
      if (Object.values(score).some((v) => v === undefined)) return;
      const total = (score.camel ?? 0) + (score.milk ?? 0) + (score.ladder ?? 0) + (score.red ?? 0);
      $('finale').classList.remove('hidden');
      $('finale-text').innerHTML = `${total}/14 judgments right, ${drash} coins for expounding and ${prisha} for withdrawing. Shimon HaAmasoni would say both count.`;
    }

    setCoins();
    renderVerse();
    renderProof();
    renderBaraita();
  </script>
</Layout>
